WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.560
You’d probably never notice a tiny 
jumping spider in your living room,

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:06.259
but it’d surely notice you.

00:00:06.259 --> 00:00:07.539
While the arachnids are famous

00:00:07.539 --> 00:00:10.259
for their excellent vision
and vibration detection,

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.740
new research shows that despite
their lack of ear drums,

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:15.240
they can also hear sounds.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.920
Researchers discovered this
by putting tiny electrodes

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.359
in the sound-sensitive parts
of the spiders’ brains

00:00:20.359 --> 00:00:22.119
and placing them on a metal box

00:00:22.119 --> 00:00:24.859
that cancelled out
vibrations from below.

00:00:24.859 --> 00:00:28.179
Then they played a sound at the
same frequency as the buzz of a wasp

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:30.420
that preys upon jumping spiders.

00:00:30.429 --> 00:00:32.909
Not only did the spiders
freeze and look around

00:00:32.909 --> 00:00:35.469
just as they’d do when
startled in the wild,

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:37.790
but data from the electrode
showed brain activity

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:40.400
spiked in the auditory area, too.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.640
This shows they actually hear sounds,

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.800
from a wasp dive-bombing

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.900
to you crunching a potato chip.

